#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147326 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying the Equilibrium Points of a Given Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "eed50b24-e97c-51c9-b625-3327783b6374" :question/math-academy-id "e-8536" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of $y'= y^3-2y^2?$ \n\n- $y=0$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a node\n\n- $y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a source\n\n- $y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a sink" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $y-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (2,\\infty),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty,0) \\cup (0,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/420aaceb6f78b8a6aa55f8e0a2a9a87e604875180a9321ff34bd72ca2384ec09.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=0$ is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\n- $y=2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a source\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147327 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"}] :question/id #uuid "dba1deed-4311-54fe-a690-8d89d4cb9592" :question/math-academy-id "e-8537" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y= -2$ is a source\n\n- $y= 3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/07fcd4a3db9ed24d7e0c391095e5950c11be4b5dac2dcc5b4cda229d69f2aaa4.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/0af6403f55ca8193993fee474183676ad4edfb0b99b4bf4707993ff512700358.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/3faccf2319817824a473b127ee7eff831ae7de82d9198b541b4d89dd7ef58ec2.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147328 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "7aa0a0a8-05d4-532c-a830-f1df43514fdf" :question/math-academy-id "e-8538" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/87dbc31b08ad8735b7a8e9f18bc74c448a221fe665acca3d40208786f20459ce.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y= -3$ is a sink.\n\n- $y= -1$ is a node.\n\n- $y= 2$ is a source." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/413ddd3248fb53bbd623794970ee2b4a561d17e2659bb72cf864436d9fffa792.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147330 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/id #uuid "bb252f4f-ef82-52bc-a485-f88f0de96173" :question/math-academy-id "e-8650" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/f495896e6df702a60eea6825d7d72c31e978d92d68ec7b1779fe565a8dc9b946.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=1$ is a source\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink\n\n- $y=-2$ is a node" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=1$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from  $y=1.$ This means that $y = 1$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side of $y=-2.$ This means that $y=-2$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbb76991-3ccc-5dad-ba2c-7057ef47f855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318144} {:answer/id #uuid "ce36ff0f-176d-566a-b1d3-bc3595494a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318145} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad36393-ca24-55da-95b9-526ec97cbbf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318146} {:answer/id #uuid "411b4708-72dc-5cb1-9178-e1e2bf8f8334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318147} {:answer/id #uuid "da2709e4-22eb-557f-975d-cf5045eac40d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "411b4708-72dc-5cb1-9178-e1e2bf8f8334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318147} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d5e56fd-d319-5cad-850c-36d9accff232" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0b3584d-b4da-5f1a-a987-21ad3fd100ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-339664" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/528bf1bc9d6177f031b8b5504641903a11dfc2c2576e4759bf4cb7c278d0585c.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=0$ is a sink\n- $y=3$ is a source\n- $y=3$ is a node" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=0$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=0.$ This means that $y=0$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=3$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from $y=3.$ This means that $y=3$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376100 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6330cb7b-5a4a-5e86-9c57-f076cf053b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb