answer/id #uuid "5fd8f14d-2fc8-549a-8b79-a766a644a134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376109} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5124b5-2316-5d80-b0ac-6a54ba65ca89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376110} {:answer/id #uuid "e7790410-4b02-5f52-98fd-6c0d31b227a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376111} {:answer/id #uuid "52c0b255-d908-5577-9904-274eb32b9f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376112} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf13bc1-9dc5-5045-bfa1-22175245e109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52c0b255-d908-5577-9904-274eb32b9f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376112} :answer-field/id #uuid "87f3885f-d5ff-5d88-a6a1-e132c9f4c7b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a32fe0f-d9ac-54ba-ba09-0ca6f2b005e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-339711" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/c7ddb28ee5184cc0cc6dc0b7253d3dd3ed24481c8433e5937532345adb4162e3.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=1$ is a source.\n- $y=-2$ is a sink.\n- $y=-2$ is a node." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/f82ff6ec76ab3cf26d2da516b6bf5c5d9773824043a38a26c8db8a16150bfc86.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=1$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=-2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying the Equilibrium Points of a Given Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b1ad301-b613-5f37-abdc-b31222f32b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-4$ and $y=3$ are both nodes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376116} {:answer/id #uuid "7fbf500b-2d85-50cf-873f-c5be838b86f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-4$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376117} {:answer/id #uuid "85d4ae47-dfc9-57c5-94cc-5b62373f1e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-4$ and $y=3$ are both sinks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376118} {:answer/id #uuid "ab29185a-3f7d-5687-9b73-c87bf119d86d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-4$ is a source, and $y=3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376119} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b012bc-8824-524f-83d7-793026b25963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-4$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fbf500b-2d85-50cf-873f-c5be838b86f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-4$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376117} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b248ce1-1d54-5682-92d0-3e8187a3f079" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e59e11c9-19f2-5fd2-8af6-91ba29833c0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-340352" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+y-12?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+y-12 & =0 \\\\ (y+4)(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-4$ and $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-4,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/c2bb2cbc11afb9bb2b25fb2105d19c2748541592c77bcecab342db69834b4fe2.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-4$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-4$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82f05d1c-61d9-5838-994e-c7feddc4294b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/q-96557-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147020} {:answer/id #uuid "ba7562c8-0d8c-5377-b4fa-8f1a84574df7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/q-96557-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147021} {:answer/id #uuid "d3507101-2ea6-55cd-b72b-689dff9a483c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/q-96557-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147022} {:answer/id #uuid "e430f9b3-8406-5868-9839-7c38b88b3ca0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/q-96557-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147023} {:answer/id #uuid "f156581e-ab14-556a-a58e-32614ea1c130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/q-96557-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f156581e-ab14-556a-a58e-32614ea1c130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/q-96557-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147024} :answer-field/id #uuid "12b37c90-4285-5e39-920a-ef7eba0ca48e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a2ea5b9-6708-5e78-bf2a-f9715bfa99e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-96557" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=0$ is a source\n\n- $y=2$ is a node\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just below $y=2$ points upward, the arrow just above it points upward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/70c6b5e466eb64695ec099bba5ee02fbacf89c60b0eac48ebdb4dbe47735bf14.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/f3789101292914032885458730bfaabf42e75a9447f43a1e7409c15fb8cbbaef.png)\n\n \n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking above the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking below the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9328e16e-9916-52b9-bd60-693348c5eda2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147026} {:answer/id #uuid "dfab5c44-428b-5c0c-a0ce-428531b48567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147027} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb78471-e403-5061-aa64-a3e8fb8c2ca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147028} {:answer/id #uuid "46abd4d9-c693-5489-b647-763bf2aabe19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147029} {:answer/id #uuid "71ef255f-2d94-59f3-a224-e8011f0f15ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46abd4d9-c693-5489-b647-763bf2aabe19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147029} :answer-field/id #uuid "48c22f42-8a2f-5b9a-b085-80bd413e8101" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4de3adcb-e14e-526f-9d74-b55f67fd1c9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96558" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/9230d847daa518edfaad43f6552a48c2b3cac9523a1e9ecad8624851080e00ea.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-1$ is a source\n- $y=2$ is a sink\n- $y=2$ is a node" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-1$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from  $y=-1.$ This means that $y=-1$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=2.$This means that $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e749cb57-30c6-5395-b47b-a0225bdfcd37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318179} {:answer/id #uuid "d29d8dc6-4d9d-50d1-9af3-9b56a92cb164" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318180} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7c6b48-de2e-5567-bb55-65836844392f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318181} {:answer/id #uuid "1b6bee35-d330-5862-b8fd-88c16f973518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318182} {:answer/id #uuid "fd566b48-7f8d-5c4b-a305-4a78269892cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d29d8dc6-4d9d-50d1-9af3-9b56a92cb164" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318180} :answer-field/id #uuid "2abae91b-859d-546d-8d28-dab2e012b169" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18d0fac2-3f3b-5fc0-81b1-b5cedbfc4a46" :question/math-academy-id "q-98700" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/4c4ef140c85eb91bd01688cc2a447f13a21c2a51964c6d101887d15045cee95d.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink\n- $y=-2$ is a source\n- $y=2$ is a node" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=-2.$This means that $y=-2$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side of $y=2.$ This means that $y=2$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cdb5365-37b8-5e10-abab-3ed5aea7913f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376123} {:answer/id #uuid "7e91829d-269a-5440-900b-954af57aad6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376124} {:answer/id #uuid "ccebaa76-6612-51b5-ac19-6fba9a705671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376125} {:answer/id #uuid "3a37f389-5de9-5ea4-abda-592603270275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376126} {:answer/id #uuid "25b7d3f7-5c44-53f1-9084-c82c02da73b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cdb5365-37b8-5e10-abab-3ed5aea7913f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376123} :answer-field/id #uuid "a55eae6f-a5be-5006-b2b3-0b1d261ca5c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0aa52cf3-fa84-5d4b-a304-ec0efb6d37ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-98701" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/74790c4dbcb2d9cebee802dd155b5fa0a94e442f9bbc59c6277eb0d6fca3d60a.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-2$ is a source\n- $y=-5$ is a node\n- $y=-5$ is a sink" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from  $y=-2.$ This means that $y=-2$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-5$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=-5.$ This means that $y=-5$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10709a2d-63e2-5053-9dcc-f712e0fc9edf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376130} {:answer/id #uuid "f8863a23-1c5f-5ffa-a305-8264321a8e7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376131} {:answer/id #uuid "8d590707-5683-54b1-aa50-dced45d3c1ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376132} {:answer/id #uuid "93c86398-e49e-5d7a-84f1-359a7233c575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376133} {:answer/id #uuid "847e8962-ec98-5c24-bf60-26a29774b1bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d590707-5683-54b1-aa50-dced45d3c1ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376132} :answer-field/id #uuid "53f18f4c-531e-56b5-9105-65fe30dd7f01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb8a97a4-63f4-59b5-8c9f-9eb6032478d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-98702" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/68eb28d31145fe38d481cb2273bc35edc80bfccf1b80b2e9a75b4136eab45629.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=4$ is a source\n- $y=4$ is a node\n- $y=1$ is a sink" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=4$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from  $y=4.$ This means that $y=4$ is a source.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=1$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side oft $y=1.$This means that $y=1$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70100f06-56dd-5f91-9ea1-c05163b336ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318200} {:answer/id #uuid "4d67a8ab-7c36-581f-be90-fefbb9eb5c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318201} {:answer/id #uuid "15f527b7-3019-5235-b977-583168af8f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318202} {:answer/id #uuid "e751a523-e83d-5f62-a55c-7d20e69f7d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318203} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd7fdcc-bc9b-5382-81eb-7cf4a201eea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15f527b7-3019-5235-b977-583168af8f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318202} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c99b2f7-05c8-5c7e-b56c-4c32b3445515" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93b52619-a3ee-55f5-8942-7104450a0db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-98715" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/4a2c0103fb348883b05692721162ffba17758beb0e6f944deb328a506fed8d21.png)\n\nConsiderer the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=4$ is a source.\n- $y=2$ is a node.\n- $y=-1$ is a sink." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/df340c40982e6d22ef8a815bf043f84fd78b43b4ddc1763845d03d4b2c793aee.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=4$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a source.\n\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b70ad7f-1fe9-5b11-a0d5-ace26bf2aa94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318207} {:answer/id #uuid "78e38bb7-38ae-5c70-8810-997bf249da59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318208} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b6c43b-d176-5602-9953-4c6b81f061d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318209} {:answer/id #uuid "2836ffe3-3d50-5f28-854e-f1df42512859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318210} {:answer/id #uuid "8908aead-f581-59e9-a80d-2d52eb89778a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5b6c43b-d176-5602-9953-4c6b81f061d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318209} :answer-field/id #uuid "04cce166-b33d-540b-92e7-c67589729173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ece7eaa-a6b2-5c98-ac8f-17a221444654" :question/math-academy-id "q-98752" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/3114d1c94b63a2506a66e701346bc7367b901659b78a2cf7c191648c96a716dc.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=3$ is a node.\n- $y=-1$ is a sink.\n- $y=-1$ is a source." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/f72f89ecad74cc708b5950763002757d9d012d3859c7a5bd5eb19ae60fc54e55.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=3$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying the Equilibrium Points of a Given Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55758d1e-d33b-5677-8be9-3f33f97c7154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a source, and $y=3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318228} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9a6079-f384-506c-8a9c-c8afe0517518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318229} {:answer/id #uuid "42b4755d-8763-5111-ac50-ca89e32e4d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318230} {:answer/id #uuid "7d606246-48cf-5740-bc21-e71d80e0e300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318231} {:answer/id #uuid "270a9c82-ba27-54ff-8b05-bf68acf4d9e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a9a6079-f384-506c-8a9c-c8afe0517518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318229} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ec143c-b984-58eb-93ce-e72cae8bb019" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1da3364-5c3f-500b-bc7c-7a5f92c432cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-98760" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=-(y-3)({y}^{2}+6y+9)?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ -(y-3)({y}^{2}+6y+9) & =0 \\\\ -(y-3)(y+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-3,3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-3$ and $y=3.$\n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y-3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $(y+3)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3),$ and negative for $y\\in (3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/3c118c900486d8561864c88e1b25a461c8f459f810bb7b753d30d9d0d4780913.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-3$  is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a sink\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying the Equilibrium Points of a Given Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac9fc4e-c55c-5f62-b3e5-7f438f2a2922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376137} {:answer/id #uuid "bbee1050-db00-5617-9d18-842b1d40cce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-1$ and $y=2$ are both sinks" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376138} {:answer/id #uuid "609e3f91-a0a8-5b98-b6bd-b04e9fb74760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376139} {:answer/id #uuid "e87ddba3-fdd2-593f-9d17-72b4c80ffd0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-1$ and $y=2$ are both sources" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376140} {:answer/id #uuid "3a7ca8a5-0e83-5f5c-8e2a-bf764acf3c01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-1$ is a sink, and $y=2$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "609e3f91-a0a8-5b98-b6bd-b04e9fb74760" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376139} :answer-field/id #uuid "88fb2801-d362-51d7-9bac-cb9f73be7aa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376136}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26ed5480-33c8-5f24-be06-ea5c8236ac1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98762" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=2+y-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ 2+y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ -({y}^{2}-y-2) & =0 \\\\ -(y+1)(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y+1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and positive for $y\\in (-1,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/9230d847daa518edfaad43f6552a48c2b3cac9523a1e9ecad8624851080e00ea.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-1$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\n- $y=2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying the Equilibrium Points of a Given Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bcf6f5e-187a-5faf-9b08-2811fdecda6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318235} {:answer/id #uuid "c6838c90-d12a-5863-96d3-0456aebfc28c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318236} {:answer/id #uuid "01fe33c8-1b70-519a-a364-f3103a3bd5ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318237} {:answer/id #uuid "06f21726-8030-5fc9-a7a9-091cd8b409d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node and $y=3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318238} {:answer/id #uuid "036a74ed-c94c-585f-816f-3ceff587c392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node and $y=3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bcf6f5e-187a-5faf-9b08-2811fdecda6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318235} :answer-field/id #uuid "c19fbb3d-1027-58c8-9c0d-fef11e4f804c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f6c3551-aac2-5585-bcd3-44729e24f08b" :question/math-academy-id "q-98763" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+6y+9?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+6y+9 & =0 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=-3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $(y+3)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/074ed04c41f20a891678d61a26ae06f1151299110dfd13d2c8b7f6eef3e6e363.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-3$ is a node\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376142 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying the Equilibrium Points of a Given Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5efec162-dfd7-5d56-8603-5427baed3bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-2$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376144} {:answer/id #uuid "a3461d9d-84d3-53b6-b0d4-708719f99a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-2$ is a sink and $y=2$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376145} {:answer/id #uuid "d06eae3a-ee13-5a52-9291-9ad56da88bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-2$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376146} {:answer/id #uuid "b63fcaaf-89e8-5a4f-9093-3e9c557b7860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-2$ is a source and $y=2$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376147} {:answer/id #uuid "9cea031e-d5f4-523c-a0a2-20858738ac67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-2$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5efec162-dfd7-5d56-8603-5427baed3bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-2$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376144} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cb6e6b4-dbda-5053-981d-78e441cb259b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d8bc14b-d6cd-5708-b7ce-c5b5f753bf7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98764" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=(y+2)({y}^{2}+2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y+2)({y}^{2}+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=-2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}+2$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (2,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/ac2d3df6e9caa672e16f3499502bba9e17c5d79dd200055319c22bc29fb78340.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-2$ is a source\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318240 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f47973-dc40-5a25-bfe7-4ec27d53d565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/a375a537b104f5bcdd8f5951277bce2494d8861a7212772a7d7050fa14091cb9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318242} {:answer/id #uuid "6c326e90-6df7-57bc-a33b-babe0a3f367c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/c7895d3f3db254736c4e20ff30dbe92fb7c0786291b1198ed3f727971268be31.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318243} {:answer/id #uuid "1ccc9153-04b8-5c60-8a77-dbb5e48ff9b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/d72110682c1959f10546b6fa49d6e4ebedf6b6d5557639c772f28a920f306df5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318244} {:answer/id #uuid "4b513102-c6f7-578d-ad85-e594a933be90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/41f32a097325dbe2a278da2abe4ef93b13d058ffe49431882fade3bc40bc0f48.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318245} {:answer/id #uuid "22ddb781-c3a2-5f6e-a938-f69e1376906c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/31519aa878fd6a9b450296552afd9bbf628e30db68a2d7c6dc6b02a48ae28b06.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b513102-c6f7-578d-ad85-e594a933be90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/41f32a097325dbe2a278da2abe4ef93b13d058ffe49431882fade3bc40bc0f48.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318245} :answer-field/id #uuid "911d25e4-075e-5940-968d-92f845da40f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df1a08a1-49b6-5687-bf86-dc8094436b8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98774" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=0$ is a source\n\n- $y=-4$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-4$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-4$ (already drawn by the above property) and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/e5ba765b9caffab904f07c5086d3206f72e36e66cb018673ce7ed44d79d19e88.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/41f32a097325dbe2a278da2abe4ef93b13d058ffe49431882fade3bc40bc0f48.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=-4$ appear to converge toward this line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54c1869d-478b-587d-b16e-4d2d7d5b8c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/8289068f50fdec4729cf2a2c402040b3ec162d2769e27cd7605a6b834e513a4c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376151} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9dee5a-0605-52b0-bdd2-d2adc8422aa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/83e01284a8016cecf4cc0262c83269948bc089c5b890c6122909dc39c730dd9b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376152} {:answer/id #uuid "43bf8b08-d2cc-5db0-9bb0-8174adeb27c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/ad11e5508156e7b17e0c3ff743af19fb4d25ac0746315687cc45ee4bcca46960.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376153} {:answer/id #uuid "081711a9-40b2-57f2-a704-90fbccc54d58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/a519627a8061e39559b35506ef44ef44ee32c01aa32d8d21157261f14cb94bd4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376154} {:answer/id #uuid "255f3643-a7ca-5f16-a68b-cb221d6ec4c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/890c699885c8694597912bd65044fde50dd99ac8757578dc0db71bdb413b5db7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "255f3643-a7ca-5f16-a68b-cb221d6ec4c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/890c699885c8694597912bd65044fde50dd99ac8757578dc0db71bdb413b5db7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376155} :answer-field/id #uuid "48fa8267-7bac-5441-b8af-ca4e760b7164" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "827b522e-f596-5ffe-8bdf-3d2cacb89e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-98790" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a source\n\n- $y=2$ is a node\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-3$ and $y=2.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-3$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-3$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just below $y=2$ points upward, the arrow just above it points upward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/d03ab1d15be3656787d7ccd0114b2011f561bfed9c67d22f3366f50ed2f410e4.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/890c699885c8694597912bd65044fde50dd99ac8757578dc0db71bdb413b5db7.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-3$ and $y=2.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-3$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking above the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking below the line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "228ba247-55c9-59c7-b24c-8437fe676fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/a7961c00b93091e3bf775f6f61f503d9f3d16ad30174c4f3310654f5aa75721c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376158} {:answer/id #uuid "0a059886-3fef-54e8-b1a1-ee91db98426b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/f1302588da5388ebfa996005675625f98a9edee7c5ec0e99a2c897e70bd7e680.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376159} {:answer/id #uuid "02d24914-7b96-52c2-af1b-ebe93891dde5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/7df58db225ecb32e6b51b2c16d78a557ca57e63f5d85551dfc1ddc85865e9ea4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376160} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf34c76-9a6b-55f7-91c3-b3b90e308e8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/5737491c0a472a12cb3fd9e0b09cb5ede88b193a3f084ca89671e4669b945d5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376161} {:answer/id #uuid "12bfde5c-5dff-5290-aa13-8a8176435aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/4e360e24357a714978fdfb64b239381b3d7c66d13a1307c265f067881aaebc54.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cf34c76-9a6b-55f7-91c3-b3b90e308e8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/5737491c0a472a12cb3fd9e0b09cb5ede88b193a3f084ca89671e4669b945d5b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376161} :answer-field/id #uuid "066a9351-b2a2-5970-8d1f-ebf0bdbaa5a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a90c5eac-a818-5712-a0c3-9443f22bd769" :question/math-academy-id "q-98809" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a node\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-2$ points upward, the arrow just below it points upward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/5ea8af3b30df51189c3511dbe093f05b309dc50cbdea1e52d5ba3a42d88101c2.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/5737491c0a472a12cb3fd9e0b09cb5ede88b193a3f084ca89671e4669b945d5b.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking above the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking below the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Slope Fields Corresponding to Some Equilibrium Point Classifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "997399f0-925a-5c8f-ace4-5d78470b3e52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/q-98811-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147074} {:answer/id #uuid "b5acf733-265f-5438-b980-a858541be544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/q-98811-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147075} {:answer/id #uuid "1d9a7822-23ae-5632-8a20-2b547498ec0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/q-98811-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147076} {:answer/id #uuid "258ed779-50f1-521d-9dc8-17d86ffa3684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/q-98811-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147077} {:answer/id #uuid "61cc2e38-a7db-5d9e-9599-3bfb95c8a016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/q-98811-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "258ed779-50f1-521d-9dc8-17d86ffa3684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2533/Source/Images/q-98811-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147077} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a4807be-6348-5710-b770-10e19a33d53c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b626551c-31fe-589e-860a-053b0e741b0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98811" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a source\n\n- $y=0$ is a sink\n\n- $y=2$ is a source\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=2.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n- We're given that $y=2$ is also a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/b5afe944331145c5253b2f1100f642b8a91a538295f3c84877eeb86870a04915.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/fb27ad885fc87fa56a04ec92d46173c98fda170f11117c4748c21a67faa9e166.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=2.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=2$ appear to diverge away from this line."}]}
